Good progress - 14 out of 15 scenarios pass! The only failure is in "Toggle-all updates when individual todos change". 

The scenario is:
```
Given I have todos "Buy milk", "Buy eggs", "Buy bread"
When I complete the first 2 todos
Then the toggle-all checkbox is not checked
When I complete the last todo
Then the toggle-all checkbox is checked
```

The step "I complete the first 2 todos" uses the step definition that finds items in the `.todo-list li` and clicks their toggle checkboxes. But after clicking each one, `render()` is called which re-renders the list. The issue might be that after clicking the first toggle, the list re-renders, and then the selector for "first 2" changes.

Wait, the step definition for "I complete the first {int} todos" does:
```js
const items = this.document.querySelectorAll('.todo-list li');
for (let i = 0; i < count && i < items.length; i++) {
  const toggle = items[i].querySelector('.toggle');
  if (toggle && !items[i].classList.contains('completed')) {
    toggle.click();
  }
}
```

But each `toggle.click()` triggers the app's change handler which calls `render()`, which clears and re-renders the todo-list. After the first click, the `.todo-list li` collection is invalidated. But since we're iterating over the NodeList from the first querySelectorAll, it should be a static list... actually, in happy-dom, querySelectorAll might return a live NodeList or a static one.

But the bigger issue is that `render()` re-creates all the DOM elements. The toggle element from the first iteration is removed from the DOM when render() is called. So the second iteration's `items[i]` might have the wrong index.

Let me fix the app.js so that render is NOT called after each individual toggle change. Instead, the click handler should just update the model, and render should be called once at the end.

