Wait, there's a problem. The `seedData` parameter is used to explicitly seed data, and when it's not provided (auto-capture), I now set it to an object with multiple keys. But the step "Given I have a todo {string} with id {string}" also calls `loadApp()` with no argument, which means the auto-capture will try to capture from the previous window which may have the seeded data plus the filter.

But there's a subtle issue: when the auto-capture captures data into `seedData` as an object, and then `loadApp` is called again without arguments, the auto-capture code runs again... but `seedData` is a local variable, it won't be the same as the parameter `seedData`. Wait, actually the parameter `seedData` IS a local variable. Let me re-check.

Looking at my code:
```js
async loadApp(seedData) {
    ...
    // Capture localStorage before disposing the old window
    if (this.window && seedData === undefined) {
      try {
        var data = {};
        ...
        if (Object.keys(data).length > 0) seedData = data;
      } catch (e) { ... }
    }
    ...
    // restore persisted data into the new window before evaling the app
    if (seedData !== undefined) {
      if (typeof seedData === 'string') {
        this.window.localStorage.setItem('todos-vanilla', seedData);
      } else {
        Object.keys(seedData).forEach(function(k) {
          this.window.localStorage.setItem(k, seedData[k]);
        }.bind(this));
      }
    }
```

When `seedData` is auto-captured as an object like `{'todos-vanilla': '[...]', 'todos-vanilla-filter': 'active'}`, the restore code handles it correctly by iterating over all keys.

When the step "Given I have a todo with id" calls `this.loadApp()` after manually setting up localStorage on the old window, the auto-capture will capture both `todos-vanilla` (with the seeded data) and `todos-vanilla-filter` (from the render/save that ran during load). This should work.

But wait, there's a potential issue: when `loadApp()` is called the very first time (from Before hook), there's no old window. The `this.window` check prevents auto-capture. Then after the app loads, `render()` runs which calculates the filter and calls `save()`, which saves the filter. So the filter key is in localStorage.

